                    </input>
                </sage>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

    </subsection>

    <reading-questions xml:id="functions-reading-questions">
        <title>Reading Questions</title>

        <exercise label="rq-functions-purpose">
            <statement>
                <p>A MATLAB function is most useful when you want to:</p>
            </statement>
            <choices randomize="yes">
                <choice>
                    <statement><p>clear the Command Window automatically</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>That is done with <pf>clc</pf>, not a function you write.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
                <choice>
                    <statement><p>store a long list of comments</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>Comments describe code but do not execute; this is not the purpose of a function.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
                <choice correct="yes">
                    <statement><p>create a reusable tool that takes inputs and returns outputs</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>Correct. Functions encapsulate computations so you can call them repeatedly with different inputs.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
                <choice>
                    <statement><p>avoid using variables</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>Functions use variables heavily — both for inputs and intermediate calculations.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
            </choices>
        </exercise>

        <exercise label="rq-functions-file-name-match">
            <statement correct="yes">
                <p>If a function is named <pf>coin_total</pf>, then its file should be saved as <pf>coin_total.m</pf>.</p>
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>Matching the file name to the function name prevents "function not found" errors.</p>
            </feedback>
        </exercise>

        <exercise label="rq-functions-private-workspace">
            <statement correct="no">
                <p>Variables created inside a function automatically appear in the Workspace after the function finishes.</p>
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>A function uses its own private workspace, so its internal variables do not become Workspace variables.</p>
            </feedback>
        </exercise>

        <exercise label="rq-functions-call-three-inputs">
            <statement>
                <p>Which of the following is a valid function call with three inputs?</p>
            </statement>
            <choices randomize="yes">
                <choice><statement><p><pf>f = 2, 3, 4</pf></p></statement><feedback><p>This is an assignment, not a function call.</p></feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p><pf>f(2; 3; 4)</pf></p></statement><feedback><p>Semicolons are not used to separate function arguments.</p></feedback></choice>
                <choice correct="yes"><statement><p><pf>f(2, 3, 4)</pf></p></statement><feedback><p>Correct. Inputs are listed inside parentheses separated by commas.</p></feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p><pf>f = (2, 3, 4)</pf></p></statement><feedback><p>This is not valid MATLAB syntax.</p></feedback></choice>
            </choices>
        </exercise>

        <exercise label="rq-functions-two-output-syntax">
            <statement>
                <p>To store two outputs from a function, you should write:</p>
            </statement>
            <choices randomize="yes">
                <choice><statement><p><pf>a, b = f(x)</pf></p></statement><feedback><p>This is not valid MATLAB syntax.</p></feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p><pf>(a, b) = f(x)</pf></p></statement><feedback><p>Parentheses are not used on the left side for capturing outputs.</p></feedback></choice>
                <choice correct="yes"><statement><p><pf>[a, b] = f(x)</pf></p></statement><feedback><p>Correct. Square brackets on the left side capture multiple outputs.</p></feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p><pf>a = f(x), b</pf></p></statement><feedback><p>This does not capture both outputs correctly.</p></feedback></choice>
            </choices>
        </exercise>

        <exercise label="rq-functions-header-output-symbol">
            <statement>
                <p>In the header <pf>function y = f(x)</pf>, which symbol represents the output?</p>
            </statement>
            <choices randomize="yes">
                <choice><statement><p><pf>f</pf></p></statement><feedback><p><pf>f</pf> is the function name.</p></feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p><pf>x</pf></p></statement><feedback><p><pf>x</pf> is the input.</p></feedback></choice>
                <choice correct="yes"><statement><p><pf>y</pf></p></statement><feedback><p>Correct. The output variable appears between <pf>function</pf> and the <pf>=</pf>.</p></feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p><pf>function</pf></p></statement><feedback><p><pf>function</pf> is the keyword that begins every function definition.</p></feedback></choice>
            </choices>
        </exercise>

        <exercise label="rq-functions-calling-from-script">
            <statement correct="yes">
                <p>A script can call a function you wrote (for example, <pf>coin_total</pf>), as long as the function file is available in the Current Folder or on the path.</p>
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>This is one of the main benefits of functions: they simplify scripts by packaging common computations.</p>
            </feedback>
        </exercise>

        <exercise label="rq-functions-tilde-ignore">
            <statement>
                <p>If a function returns two outputs but you only need the second one, you can write:</p>
            </statement>
            <choices randomize="yes">
                <choice correct="yes"><statement><p><pf>[~, b] = f(x)</pf></p></statement><feedback><p>Correct. The tilde <pf>~</pf> is a placeholder that discards the unwanted output.</p></feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p><pf>[_, b] = f(x)</pf></p></statement><feedback><p>Underscore is not used as a placeholder in MATLAB; use <pf>~</pf> instead.</p></feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p><pf>b = f(x)</pf></p></statement><feedback><p>This only captures the first output, not the second.</p></feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p><pf>[null, b] = f(x)</pf></p></statement><feedback><p><pf>null</pf> is not the placeholder for ignored outputs in MATLAB.</p></feedback></choice>
            </choices>
        </exercise>

        <exercise label="rq-functions-script-vs-function-workspace">
            <statement>
                <p>Drag each description to the correct concept.</p>
            </statement>
            <cardsort>
                <match>
                    <premise>Uses the base workspace</premise>
                    <premise>Often written as a one-time recipe</premise>
                    <response>Script</response>
                </match>
                <match>
                    <premise>Has its own private workspace</premise>
                    <premise>Designed for reuse with many inputs</premise>
                    <response>Function</response>
                </match>
            </cardsort>
            <feedback>
                <p>Scripts and functions both run top-to-bottom, but functions emphasize reusable input/output behavior and a separate workspace.</p>
            </feedback>
        </exercise>

        <exercise label="rq-functions-where-to-save">
            <statement>
                <p>For MATLAB to find your function when you call it, where should you save the <pf>.m</pf> file?</p>
            </statement>
            <choices randomize="yes">
                <choice correct="yes"><statement><p>In the Current Folder or in a folder on the MATLAB path</p></statement><feedback><p>Correct. MATLAB searches the Current Folder and the path when resolving function names.</p></feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p>In the Documents folder</p></statement><feedback><p>MATLAB does not automatically search the Documents folder unless it is on the path.</p></feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p>In any folder on your computer</p></statement><feedback><p>MATLAB only searches the Current Folder and folders listed on the path.</p></feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p>In the same folder as MATLAB itself</p></statement><feedback><p>You should not place your files in the MATLAB installation directory.</p></feedback></choice>
            </choices>
        </exercise>

        <exercise label="rq-functions-fail-a-zero">
            <statement>
                <p>Fill in the blank: The quadratic formula function <pf>[x1,x2] = quadratic(a,b,c)</pf> will not work correctly (it divides by zero and produces Inf or NaN) when <pf>a</pf> equals <fillin answer="0"/>.</p>
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>When <pf>a = 0</pf>, the formula divides by <m>2a = 0</m>, which is mathematically undefined. In MATLAB, this division by zero returns special values like <pf>Inf</pf> or <pf>NaN</pf> (often with a warning), so a robust quadratic function should detect this case with a conditional statement and handle it separately.</p>
            </feedback>
        </exercise>

        <exercise label="rq-functions-end-keyword">
            <statement correct="yes">
                <p>Every MATLAB function definition should conclude with the keyword <pf>end</pf>.</p>
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>While MATLAB can sometimes infer the end of a function from the file boundary, using <pf>end</pf> explicitly is required when multiple functions appear in one file and is considered best practice.</p>
            </feedback>
        </exercise>

    </reading-questions>

    <exercises xml:id="functions-coding-exercises">
        <title>Coding Exercises</title>

        <exercise xml:id="coding-celsius-to-fahrenheit">
            <title>Temperature Conversion</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Write a function named <pf>celsius_to_fahrenheit</pf> that converts a temperature from Celsius to Fahrenheit.
                    The conversion formula is: <m>F = \frac{9}{5}C + 32</m>.
                </p>
                <tabular valign="top" top="minor">
                    <col width="11%" halign="right" /><col width="15%" halign="left" />
                    <col width="28%" halign="center" /><col width="46%" halign="left"/>
                    <row><cell>Inputs:</cell><cell><p><pf>celsius</pf></p></cell><cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell><cell><p>temperature in degrees Celsius</p></cell></row>
                    <row bottom="minor"><cell>Outputs:</cell><cell><p><pf>fahrenheit</pf></p></cell><cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell><cell><p>temperature in degrees Fahrenheit</p></cell></row>
                </tabular>
                <p>Test cases:</p>
                <program language="matlab">
                    <code>
f1 = celsius_to_fahrenheit(0)    % Expected: f1 = 32
f2 = celsius_to_fahrenheit(100)  % Expected: f2 = 212
f3 = celsius_to_fahrenheit(20)   % Expected: f3 = 68
                    </code>
                </program>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <program language="matlab">
                    <code>
function fahrenheit = celsius_to_fahrenheit(celsius)
    fahrenheit = (9/5) * celsius + 32;
end
                    </code>
                </program>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="coding-triangle-area">
            <title>Triangle Area</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Write a function named <pf>triangle_area</pf> that computes the area of a triangle given its base and height.
                    The formula is: <m>A = \frac{1}{2} \times \text{base} \times \text{height}</m>.
                </p>
                <tabular valign="top" top="minor">
                    <col width="11%" halign="right" /><col width="15%" halign="left" />
                    <col width="28%" halign="center" /><col width="46%" halign="left"/>
                    <row><cell>Inputs:</cell><cell><p><pf>base</pf></p></cell><cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell><cell><p>length of the triangle's base</p></cell></row>
                    <row><cell></cell><cell><p><pf>height</pf></p></cell><cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell><cell><p>height of the triangle</p></cell></row>
                    <row bottom="minor"><cell>Outputs:</cell><cell><p><pf>area</pf></p></cell><cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell><cell><p>area of the triangle</p></cell></row>
                </tabular>
                <p>Test cases:</p>
                <program language="matlab">
                    <code>
a1 = triangle_area(6, 4)    % Expected: a1 = 12
a2 = triangle_area(5, 12)   % Expected: a2 = 30
a3 = triangle_area(7.5, 3.2) % Expected: a3 = 12
                    </code>
                </program>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <program language="matlab">
                    <code>
function area = triangle_area(base, height)
    area = 0.5 * base * height;
end
                    </code>
                </program>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="coding-sphere-properties">
            <title>Sphere Properties</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Write a function named <pf>sphere_properties</pf> that computes both the surface area and volume of a sphere given its radius.
                </p>
                <p>
                    The formulas are:
                    <ul>
                        <li><p>Surface area: <m>A = 4\pi r^2</m></p></li>
                        <li><p>Volume: <m>V = \frac{4}{3}\pi r^3</m></p></li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <tabular valign="top" top="minor">
                    <col width="11%" halign="right" /><col width="15%" halign="left" />
                    <col width="28%" halign="center" /><col width="46%" halign="left"/>
                    <row><cell>Inputs:</cell><cell><p><pf>radius</pf></p></cell><cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell><cell><p>radius of the sphere</p></cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Outputs:</cell><cell><p><pf>surface_area</pf></p></cell><cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell><cell><p>surface area of the sphere</p></cell></row>
                    <row bottom="minor"><cell></cell><cell><p><pf>volume</pf></p></cell><cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell><cell><p>volume of the sphere</p></cell></row>
                </tabular>
                <p>Test cases:</p>
                <program language="matlab">
                    <code>
[A1, V1] = sphere_properties(1)  % Expected: A1 ≈ 12.5664, V1 ≈ 4.1888
[A2, V2] = sphere_properties(2)  % Expected: A2 ≈ 50.2655, V2 ≈ 33.5103
[A3, V3] = sphere_properties(5)  % Expected: A3 ≈ 314.1593, V3 ≈ 523.5988
                    </code>
                </program>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <program language="matlab">
                    <code>
function [surface_area, volume] = sphere_properties(radius)
    surface_area = 4 * pi * radius^2;
    volume = (4/3) * pi * radius^3;
end
                    </code>
                </program>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="coding-compound-interest">
            <title>Compound Interest Calculator</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Write a function named <pf>compound_interest</pf> that calculates the future value of an investment with compound interest,
                    and also returns the total interest earned and the effective annual rate.
                </p>
                <p>
                    The compound interest formula is: <m>A = P\left(1 + \frac{r}{n}\right)^{nt}</m>,
                    where <m>P</m> is the principal, <m>r</m> is the annual rate (decimal), <m>n</m> is compounding frequency per year, and <m>t</m> is time in years.
                    The effective annual rate (EAR) is: <m>\text{EAR} = \left(1 + \frac{r}{n}\right)^n - 1</m>.
                </p>
                <tabular valign="top" top="minor">
                    <col width="11%" halign="right" /><col width="22%" halign="left" />
                    <col width="21%" halign="center" /><col width="46%" halign="left"/>
                    <row><cell>Inputs:</cell><cell><p><pf>principal</pf></p></cell><cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell><cell><p>initial investment amount</p></cell></row>
                    <row><cell></cell><cell><p><pf>rate</pf></p></cell><cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell><cell><p>annual interest rate (as decimal)</p></cell></row>
                    <row><cell></cell><cell><p><pf>compounds_per_year</pf></p></cell><cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell><cell><p>compounding frequency (e.g., 12 for monthly)</p></cell></row>
                    <row><cell></cell><cell><p><pf>years</pf></p></cell><cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell><cell><p>time period in years</p></cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Outputs:</cell><cell><p><pf>final_amount</pf></p></cell><cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell><cell><p>total amount after compound interest</p></cell></row>
                    <row><cell></cell><cell><p><pf>interest_earned</pf></p></cell><cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell><cell><p>total interest earned</p></cell></row>
                    <row bottom="minor"><cell></cell><cell><p><pf>effective_rate</pf></p></cell><cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell><cell><p>effective annual rate</p></cell></row>
                </tabular>
                <p>Test cases:</p>
                <program language="matlab">
                    <code>
% $1000 at 5% monthly for 10 years
[final1, int1, effRate1] = compound_interest(1000, 0.05, 12, 10)
% Expected: final1 ≈ 1647.01, int1 ≈ 647.01, effRate1 ≈ 0.0512
                    </code>
                </program>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <program language="matlab">
                    <code>
function [final_amount, interest_earned, effective_rate] = ...
        compound_interest(principal, rate, compounds_per_year, years)
    final_amount = principal * (1 + rate/compounds_per_year)^(compounds_per_year*years);
    interest_earned = final_amount - principal;
    effective_rate = (1 + rate/compounds_per_year)^compounds_per_year - 1;
end
                    </code>
                </program>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="coding-triangle-3d">
            <title>Triangle Perimeter and Area in 3D Space</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Write a function named <pf>triangle_3d</pf> that computes the perimeter and area of a triangle in 3D space
                    given the coordinates of its three vertices.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Hints:
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                The distance between two points <m>(x_1, y_1, z_1)</m> and <m>(x_2, y_2, z_2)</m> is:
                                <me>d = \sqrt{(x_2 - x_1)^2 + (y_2 - y_1)^2 + (z_2 - z_1)^2}</me>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Use Heron's formula with semi-perimeter <m>s = \frac{a+b+c}{2}</m>:
                                <me>A = \sqrt{s(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)}</me>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
                <tabular valign="top" top="minor">
                    <col width="11%" halign="right" /><col width="22%" halign="left" />
                    <col width="21%" halign="center" /><col width="46%" halign="left"/>
                    <row><cell>Inputs:</cell><cell><p><pf>x1, y1, z1</pf></p></cell><cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell><cell><p>coordinates of first vertex</p></cell></row>
                    <row><cell></cell><cell><p><pf>x2, y2, z2</pf></p></cell><cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell><cell><p>coordinates of second vertex</p></cell></row>
                    <row><cell></cell><cell><p><pf>x3, y3, z3</pf></p></cell><cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell><cell><p>coordinates of third vertex</p></cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Outputs:</cell><cell><p><pf>perimeter</pf></p></cell><cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell><cell><p>perimeter of the triangle</p></cell></row>
                    <row bottom="minor"><cell></cell><cell><p><pf>area</pf></p></cell><cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell><cell><p>area of the triangle</p></cell></row>
                </tabular>
                <p>Test cases:</p>
                <program language="matlab">
                    <code>
[p1, a1] = triangle_3d(0, 0, 0, 4, 0, 0, 0, 3, 0)
% Expected: p1 = 12, a1 = 6  (3-4-5 right triangle)

[p2, a2] = triangle_3d(0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0.5, sqrt(3)/2, 0)
% Expected: p2 = 3, a2 ≈ 0.4330  (equilateral)
                    </code>
                </program>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <program language="matlab">
                    <code>
function [perimeter, area] = triangle_3d(x1,y1,z1, x2,y2,z2, x3,y3,z3)
    a = sqrt((x2-x1)^2 + (y2-y1)^2 + (z2-z1)^2);
    b = sqrt((x3-x2)^2 + (y3-y2)^2 + (z3-z2)^2);
    c = sqrt((x1-x3)^2 + (y1-y3)^2 + (z1-z3)^2);
    perimeter = a + b + c;
    s = perimeter / 2;
    area = sqrt(s*(s-a)*(s-b)*(s-c));
end
                    </code>
                </program>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

    </exercises>

</section>
